as drought conditions worsen across the  valley many have reached out to us  questioning watering schedules at local  parks sally jaramillo is live near d-i  and decatur with how they operate in the  efforts to conserve sally  kirsten many of our channel a viewers  have reached out to us asking us if  parks have to also follow the same  watering schedule  as home buyers now the answer is yes but  depending on the specific park those  schedules could change  i visit local parks almost every weekend  i bring my daughter and her friends to  parks on the west side sometimes in  north summerlin or even up in north las  vegas courtney kreisberg is a mother who  lives on the west side part of the  valley she has lived here for over 20  years and says right now it is very  important for all las vegas and all  nevada residents to conserve water and  when it comes to community parks  spokesperson for the las vegas valley  water district bronson mack says parks  are permitted to submit a designation as  a community used recreational turf area  under that designation if approved they  are permitted to have an alternative  watering schedule parks are following a  watering schedule to help ensure that  they're doing exactly what residents are  doing max says the parks here are  extremely responsive we've replaced many  of our sports fields with artificial  turf senior manager analyst for the city  of las vegas marco vilada says at this  point last year they had reduced 100  million gallons of water which is about  eight percent over the previous fiscal  year we want to continue to make those  conservation measures and do things to  reduce our overall uh consumptive use  try to cut back on your water usage when  you can because the future of las vegas  depends on it  back out live if you discover water  being wasted at a park now contact the  specific municipality or you can simply  report it online for now reporting live  sally caramillo live local now
 

